{"question_id": "Q40", "system": "S4_r1", "category": "Explanation", "answer": "The earliest water-breakthrough alerts are tied on 2010-06-01, for wells 15/9-F-12 and 15/9-F-14. F-12: triggered by water_cut reading of 0.5446 on 2010-06-01 (R_water_cut_F-12_2010-06-01); recommended intervention = polymer_injection. F-14: triggered by water_cut reading on 2010-06-01 (R_water_cut_F-14_2010-06-01); recommended intervention = gas_lift_opt.", "citations": ["A_WATER_BREAKTHROUGH_F-12_2010-06-01", "R_water_cut_F-12_2010-06-01", "I_WATER_BREAKTHROUGH_F-12_2010-06-01", "A_WATER_BREAKTHROUGH_F-14_2010-06-01", "R_water_cut_F-14_2010-06-01", "I_WATER_BREAKTHROUGH_F-14_2010-06-01"], "citation_ok": true, "citation_invalid": [], "n_tool_calls": 6, "exact_match": null, "ndcg_at_5": null, "rubric": 2}
{"question_id": "Q41", "system": "S4_r1", "category": "Explanation", "answer": "F-5 \u2192 F-11 has the strongest connectivity weight (0.4368) among all of F-5's injection targets (vs. 0.2922 for F-12, 0.0803 for F-15D, 0.0728 for F-14), so a dominant share of F-5's injected water flows straight to F-11. With a short tau of 45 days, that water arrives fast, driving early breakthrough \u2014 evidenced by F-11's WATER_BREAKTHROUGH alert (2015-08-01) and the ensuing HIGH_WOR alerts.", "citations": ["W_F-5", "W_F-11", "A_WATER_BREAKTHROUGH_F-11_2015-08-01", "A_HIGH_WOR_F-11_2015-08-01"], "citation_ok": true, "citation_invalid": [], "n_tool_calls": 2, "exact_match": null, "ndcg_at_5": null, "rubric": 2}
{"question_id": "Q42", "system": "S4_r1", "category": "Explanation", "answer": "The cheapest intervention is I_WCT_SEVERE_F-14_2015-05-01 (8.28 $/bbl, well 15/9-F-14). Provenance: S_water_cut_F-14 (source) \u2192 R_water_cut_F-14_2015-05-01 (MEASURED_BY, water_cut reading) \u2192 A_WCT_SEVERE_F-14_2015-05-01 (WCT_SEVERE alert, TRIGGERED_BY reading) \u2192 I_WCT_SEVERE_F-14_2015-05-01 (RECOMMENDED_FOR alert).", "citations": ["I_WCT_SEVERE_F-14_2015-05-01", "A_WCT_SEVERE_F-14_2015-05-01", "R_water_cut_F-14_2015-05-01", "S_water_cut_F-14"], "citation_ok": true, "citation_invalid": [], "n_tool_calls": 4, "exact_match": null, "ndcg_at_5": null, "rubric": 3}
{"question_id": "Q43", "system": "S4_r1", "category": "Explanation", "answer": "A HIGH_WOR alert is supported by a log_wor MetricReading crossing above the threshold of 0 (log10 WOR > 0, i.e. WOR > 1 \u2014 water rate exceeds oil rate). Example: alert A_HIGH_WOR_F-12_2010-06-01 is TRIGGERED_BY reading R_log_wor_F-12_2010-06-01 (log_wor = 0.0778), the first month above 0; the preceding month R_log_wor_F-12_2010-05-01 (\u22120.0153, below 0) was not flagged.", "citations": ["A_HIGH_WOR_F-12_2010-06-01", "R_log_wor_F-12_2010-06-01", "R_log_wor_F-12_2010-05-01"], "citation_ok": true, "citation_invalid": [], "n_tool_calls": 4, "exact_match": null, "ndcg_at_5": null, "rubric": 0}
{"question_id": "Q44", "system": "S4_r1", "category": "Explanation", "answer": "F-12 shows a long dry plateau (<2% through late 2009) then a gradual, smooth monotonic rise to ~76% by May 2011. F-15 D is dry through 2014, creeps to ~7\u20138% by mid-2015, then has a sharp step-change breakthrough (~7.6%\u219234% Jul\u2192Aug 2015) and plateaus around ~56\u201361% into 2016 \u2014 i.e. gradual ramp (F-12) vs. abrupt breakthrough then plateau (F-15 D).", "citations": ["R_water_cut_F-12_2009-12-01", "R_water_cut_F-12_2010-04-01", "R_water_cut_F-12_2010-08-01", "R_water_cut_F-12_2011-05-01", "R_water_cut_F-15D_2014-01-01", "R_water_cut_F-15D_2015-07-01", "R_water_cut_F-15D_2015-08-01", "R_water_cut_F-15D_2015-12-01", "R_water_cut_F-15D_2016-06-01"], "citation_ok": true, "citation_invalid": [], "n_tool_calls": 4, "exact_match": null, "ndcg_at_5": null, "rubric": 2}
{"question_id": "Q45", "system": "S4_r1", "category": "Explanation", "answer": "Injector F-5 has a higher INJECTS_INTO connectivity weight to producer F-11 (0.4368) than injector F-4 does (0.3473). Since the weight measures the strength of hydraulic/pressure support conveyed from injector to producer (both with the same ~45-day lag), F-11 receives more voidage/pressure support from F-5 than from F-4 \u2014 about 26% more (0.4368 vs 0.3473).", "citations": ["W_F-11", "W_F-5", "W_F-4"], "citation_ok": true, "citation_invalid": [], "n_tool_calls": 1, "exact_match": null, "ndcg_at_5": null, "rubric": 2}
{"question_id": "Q46", "system": "S4_r1", "category": "Explanation", "answer": "The highest-risk producer is 15/9-F-14 (W_F-14): highest alert load (99 total, 24 WCT_SEVERE) and highest latest water cut (0.9662). Its water is driven by injectors F-4 (weight 0.163) and F-5 (weight 0.073) via ~45-day INJECTS_INTO links; breakthrough (2010-06) escalated to sustained WCT_SEVERE. Best recommendation = water_shutoff I_WCT_SEVERE_F-14_2016-02-01 ($8.98/bbl, 13,108 bbl uplift, top uplift-per-cost \u2248 1459.7).", "citations": ["W_F-14", "A_WCT_SEVERE_F-14_2015-11-01", "A_WATER_BREAKTHROUGH_F-14_2010-06-01", "R_water_cut_F-14_2016-07-01", "R_log_wor_F-14_2016-07-01", "I_WCT_SEVERE_F-14_2016-02-01", "I_WCT_SEVERE_F-14_2015-12-01", "I_WCT_SEVERE_F-14_2015-10-01"], "citation_ok": true, "citation_invalid": [], "n_tool_calls": 12, "exact_match": null, "ndcg_at_5": null, "rubric": 3}
{"question_id": "Q47", "system": "S4_r1", "category": "Explanation", "answer": "Every node carries source_record_id as a provenance pointer to the underlying source record it was fused from (e.g., W_F-12 \u2192 volve:well:15/9-F-12; A_WCT_SEVERE_F-12_2013-03-01 \u2192 rule:WCT_SEVERE:R_water_cut_F-12_2013-03-01). This matters because citation validation can confirm each cited node id resolves to a real, source-backed record, making facts auditable and letting fabricated/unsupported citations be rejected.", "citations": ["W_F-12", "W_F-1C", "W_F-14", "A_WCT_SEVERE_F-12_2013-03-01", "A_WATER_BREAKTHROUGH_F-12_2010-06-01"], "citation_ok": true, "citation_invalid": [], "n_tool_calls": 2, "exact_match": null, "ndcg_at_5": null, "rubric": 3}
{"question_id": "Q48", "system": "S4_r1", "category": "Explanation", "answer": "The recommended water_shutoff interventions are each triggered by water-diagnostic alerts (RECOMMENDED_FOR edge). Specifically, they are caused by severe/rising water-cut: 9 stem from WCT_SEVERE alerts (high severity, sourced from water_cut readings) on wells F-12 and F-14, and 1 from a WATER_BREAKTHROUGH alert on F-15 D \u2014 all rooted in water_cut exceeding threshold (with related HIGH_WOR / log_wor diagnostics on the same wells).", "citations": ["I_WCT_SEVERE_F-12_2013-03-01", "A_WCT_SEVERE_F-12_2013-03-01", "I_WATER_BREAKTHROUGH_F-15D_2015-12-01", "A_WATER_BREAKTHROUGH_F-12_2010-06-01", "A_WCT_SEVERE_F-12_2013-11-01", "I_WCT_SEVERE_F-14_2015-02-01", "A_HIGH_WOR_F-12_2010-06-01"], "citation_ok": true, "citation_invalid": [], "n_tool_calls": 3, "exact_match": null, "ndcg_at_5": null, "rubric": 3}
{"question_id": "Q49", "system": "S4_r1", "category": "Explanation", "answer": "Injector F-4 injects into 4 producers: F-11 (weight 0.3473, dominant), F-12 (0.2684), F-14 (0.1628), and F-1C (0.1401); all edges have tau_days=45.", "citations": ["W_F-4", "W_F-11", "W_F-12", "W_F-14", "W_F-1C"], "citation_ok": true, "citation_invalid": [], "n_tool_calls": 1, "exact_match": null, "ndcg_at_5": null, "rubric": 3}
{"question_id": "Q50", "system": "S4_r1", "category": "Explanation", "answer": "The three node types form a causal chain centred on the Alert. A MetricReading (e.g. R_water_cut_F-12_2010-06-01) is connected to the Alert via the Alert's outgoing TRIGGERED_BY edge (Alert -[TRIGGERED_BY]-> MetricReading) \u2014 i.e. the reading is the evidence that fired the rule (the Alert's source_record_id points to that same reading). The recommended Intervention links to the Alert via an Intervention -[RECOMMENDED_FOR]-> Alert edge (the intervention is proposed in response to the alert). All three hang off the same producer Well (the reading also carries a MEASURED_BY edge to its sensor/metric series, and the intervention has a FOLLOWED_BY edge to a later outcome reading). So: MetricReading (breaches threshold) \u2192 TRIGGERED_BY \u2192 Alert (raised on a Well) \u2192 RECOMMENDED_FOR \u2190 Intervention (the fix), with the intervention's effect later tracked via FOLLOWED_BY to a subsequent reading.", "citations": ["A_WATER_BREAKTHROUGH_F-12_2010-06-01", "R_water_cut_F-12_2010-06-01", "I_WATER_BREAKTHROUGH_F-12_2010-06-01", "S_water_cut_F-12", "R_np_cum_F-12_2016-09-01"], "citation_ok": true, "citation_invalid": [], "n_tool_calls": 4, "exact_match": null, "ndcg_at_5": null, "rubric": 1}
